WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:02.294
♪ (TENSE MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:02.753 --> 00:00:04.254
And we are the
Gun Trace Task Force.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.257
No matter what you're doing
out there in those streets...

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:08.550
Jackpot, motherfuckers!

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:09.885
Woo!

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:11.637
...your word prevails.

00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:16.558
GEORGE PELECANOS:
The story of We Own this City

00:00:16.725 --> 00:00:18.268
is about one of
the most corrupt squads

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:19.770
in policing history.

00:00:19.895 --> 00:00:21.522
-OFFICER: Police!
-(OFFICERS YELLING)

00:00:21.647 --> 00:00:22.898
OFFICER: Police.
Get on the ground.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:24.733
REINALDO MARCUS GREEN:
This is not just make-believe.

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:27.152
Every single story
in this series

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:29.279
was based on some
real life, true event.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.824
We Own This City is a book
by Justin Fenton.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.203
JUSTIN FENTON: Yeah,
I've been a crime reporter
in Baltimore since 2008.

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:38.580
So I had a front row seat
to all these stories.

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:40.749
D. WATKINS:
Back when I was coming up,

00:00:40.916 --> 00:00:43.835
police officers used to
pull up on a crowd of people.

00:00:44.962 --> 00:00:46.630
Jumped out of the car,
and they made all of us

00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:48.090
lie down face first
on the concrete.

00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:49.049
They all go.

00:00:49.174 --> 00:00:50.467
Then when I become a journalist,

00:00:50.842 --> 00:00:53.136
I felt compelled
to tell their stories,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.137
to tell my story,

00:00:54.221 --> 00:00:55.931
to talk about
what we've been going through.

00:00:56.682 --> 00:00:58.517
DAVID SIMON: There's something
really gratifying about

00:00:58.684 --> 00:00:59.685
working off the real.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.270
But then, it carries this...

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.146
concomitant responsibility.

00:01:03.272 --> 00:01:05.482
In the writer's room,
we had to argue out:

00:01:05.566 --> 00:01:07.609
What is it fair to say,
what is it not fair to say?

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:09.820
PELECANOS:
The pandemic put us all

00:01:09.945 --> 00:01:12.197
in a position where
we actually could take

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:13.824
a year and a half
to write this thing.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:16.201
GREEN: George Pelecanos
and David Simon,

00:01:16.285 --> 00:01:17.703
their research
is second to none.

00:01:17.786 --> 00:01:18.662
In the writer's room,

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:20.747
there was court documents
and all types of things

00:01:20.831 --> 00:01:22.249
stacked up
to the ceiling, right?

00:01:22.374 --> 00:01:24.876
PELECANOS: We tried to film
mostly in the locations

00:01:25.002 --> 00:01:26.378
where the incidents occurred.

00:01:26.503 --> 00:01:28.630
-Driver, show me your hands.
-Hands! Show me your hands!

00:01:28.714 --> 00:01:30.382
-Get out of the fucking car!
-Get out of the car!

00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:32.426
GREEN: You know where
the shellings were placed,

00:01:32.551 --> 00:01:34.011
exactly the cross streets,

00:01:34.136 --> 00:01:36.305
the type of car,
the make, the vehicle,

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:37.556
who was on the streets.

00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:40.934
(TIRES SQUEAL)

00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:43.020
It feels authentic
because it is.

00:01:43.145 --> 00:01:44.605
♪ (TENSE MUSIC CONTINUES) ♪

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.898
I'm Nicole Steele
with the Department of Justice.

00:01:46.982 --> 00:01:49.735
Nicole Steele is one of
the few people in the show

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.236
who's not based on
somebody who's real.

00:01:51.737 --> 00:01:53.614
SIMON: Nicole Steele
is a composite, really,

00:01:53.697 --> 00:01:56.742
of a number of investigators
with the civil rights division.

00:01:56.867 --> 00:01:59.202
We hold hands,
we twist arms.

00:01:59.328 --> 00:02:01.955
WANMI MOSAKU:
Ray and David and George,
we all talked about

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.543
how Nicole's character
almost feels like the audience.

00:02:06.627 --> 00:02:09.713
She's watching this
melee of madness

00:02:09.838 --> 00:02:11.298
and asking, "Why?"

00:02:12.382 --> 00:02:14.301
There was a lot of discussion
in the writer's room

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:16.887
about the limits
of the federal government's
consent decrees,

00:02:17.054 --> 00:02:19.473
given the continuing
war against drugs.

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:21.224
And through
Nicole Steele's character,

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.477
we get to see
her struggling with that.

00:02:24.561 --> 00:02:27.731
What happens if the police
don't want to change?

00:02:30.609 --> 00:02:32.069
SIMON: We really wanted
to pay attention

00:02:32.194 --> 00:02:34.738
not only to what was happening
to the Gun Trace Task Force,

00:02:34.863 --> 00:02:36.323
but what was happening
to the world around them.

00:02:36.406 --> 00:02:38.116
PROTESTERS: (CHANTING)
No justice, no peace!

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.826
No justice, no peace!

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:43.121
The conversation is bigger
than a few bad apples.

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:45.123
(YELLING)

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:47.959
The conversation should be
about a system

00:02:48.043 --> 00:02:51.129
that rewards officers
for being the bad apples.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.006
I think Baltimore
is a microcosm

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:54.633
for what's happening
all over the country.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:59.221
-DIRECTOR: And action.
-WAYNE JENKINS: On the ground.
Get down on the ground.

00:02:59.346 --> 00:03:02.057
Hey, man! That's some
great fucking police work, dog!

00:03:04.059 --> 00:03:05.310
It's yours. You earned it.

00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:09.606
(POLICE RADIO CHATTER)

